# Load Testing Rules (k6)

## Runtime — do not treat this like a Node package
- k6 scripts run inside k6's own Goja-based JS runtime, not Node. There is no `node_modules`
  and no arbitrary `npm install` at runtime — `tests/load/k6` is intentionally NOT part of the
  pnpm workspace. Shared logic (`lib/auth.js`, `lib/data.js`) uses plain relative-path ES
  module imports only.
- If a scenario needs a capability k6 does not ship with, that is an `xk6` extension/custom
  binary decision to raise explicitly — do not reach for an npm package that will not resolve.

## Three tiers — know which one you are writing
- **Smoke** (`*-smoke.js`): 1 VU, 1 iteration. Runs in PR CI on every PR touching `backend/**`.
  Functional pass/fail, not a real load test — must always pass.
- **Load** (`*-load.js`): ramping VUs, sustained hold, nightly only. Enforces thresholds via
  k6's built-in `thresholds` config so a breach fails the run automatically — do not just log
  the numbers and eyeball them.
- **Spike** (`*-spike.js`): sudden jump to ~2x normal load for a short window. Scheduled/manual
  only, informational rather than a hard merge gate.
- Default proposed thresholds (confirm/override per `docs/TEST_PLAN.md` §14 before treating as
  final): `p(95)<200ms`, `p(99)<500ms`, `http_req_failed rate<0.001`.

## Auth and test data
- Fetch a token/cookie once in `setup()`, pass it to every VU. Never let each VU log in
  independently — that turns a targeted load test into an accidental login-endpoint load test.
- Each VU/iteration generates its own unique payload via `lib/data.js`. Reusing one fixed
  project/prompt id at high RPS exercises lock contention and cache behavior that does not
  represent realistic traffic.

## Never
- Never point any scenario at a real LLM provider (Anthropic/OpenAI/Azure). Playground-adjacent
  load scenarios must run against a stubbed/mocked provider — this is a cost and rate-limit
  risk, not just a correctness concern.
- Never loosen an agreed threshold to make a run pass without raising it with the team first —
  see `docs/TEST_PLAN.md` §14 open items.

## Results
- Smoke run output uploads as a CI artifact (JSON summary).
- Once the Phase 0 observability stack exists (`docs/MICROSERVICES_PLAN.md` §9), load/spike
  runs push metrics to the same Prometheus via the `xk6-output-prometheus-remote` output, so
  results land in the existing Grafana instance rather than a separate dashboard.
- Phase 2 (`usage-service`) load runs specifically inform the WebFlux/R2DBC go/no-go call
  (`docs/MICROSERVICES_PLAN.md` §7) — always compare against the prior implementation's
  baseline numbers, not just an absolute threshold.
